Hi Eric,

Thanks for your quick reply!

To answer your questions, I am using Distance version 1.0.9,
and the issue has popped up when I had to switch between my new laptop (where there
were no errors for this analysis) and an older laptop. I don’t remember updating
any packages, but it seems that there’s a difference between the two (and the
former is being repaired, so I can’t switch back). I presume this was an issue all along, I just hadn't had the warning message before the update?

Yes, that’s correct, so it’s a primate study (multiple species)
and I’m using vegetation strata as a factor covariate. I have 288 detections
across 3 study sites (I’ve pooled this data as well as stratified it by site),
and I was at least getting the models to run as the bigger (pooled) dataset
before. Unfortunately, 56 of 288 observations are without vegetation strata
data. I originally had four categories of strata, which I have consolidated
into two broader categories (lower and upper strata) in the hope that might
help, but there are still a couple of cases where there are less than 10 detections
of one of the categories (upper strata) when looking at (stratified) study site
level.

So, am I right in thinking that the issue is both: 1. having
rows of data without a value for the strata covariate and 2. With the stratified
datasets where one strata category isn’t well represented (less than ten occurrences)?

I’m including the output you mentioned for one of my models
to see what you think (this is after collapsing the original 4 categories for strata
into 2, but still keeping the lines of data that are missing a value for strata):

Detection function parameters

Scale coefficient(s):

estimate        se

(Intercept)
3.4177687 0.3080195

StrataLower Strata -0.7480261 0.3584839

StrataUpper Strata -0.1233200 0.4132978

Please let me know what you think.

Best wishes,

Lucy

