MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Kat

I'll address your questions by the numbers you used in your email:
weights is only used when the response variable is density; as you suggested, in other situations sampled area is 2w*seglength
in nearly all circumstances, you will not use the  weights argument. The offset (used in the gam model) is calculated automatically
weights is not used when response variable is count
choice between  count and abundance.est as response variable is determined by whether individual level covariates are included in the detection function model. Individual level covariates examples: species or sex of detected animal. When those covariates are not in the detection function model, use count as response.
The choice of response variables is discussed in the  dsm case study section on model fitting <https://distancesampling.org/dsm/articles/lines_gomex/mexico-analysis.html#fitting-a-dsm>. Discussion of the use of the weights argument is found in  this section of help for dsm <https://distancesampling.org/dsm/reference/dsm.html#details>
Fit a density surface model to segment-specific estimates of abundance or density. — dsm • dsm <https://distancesampling.org/dsm/reference/dsm.html#details>
Details. The response (LHS of formula) can be one of the following (with restrictions outlined below):. count count in each segment. abundance.est estimated abundance per segment, estimation is via a Horvitz-Thompson estimator. density.est density per segment. The offset used in the model is dependent on the response: count area of segment multiplied by average probability of detection in the ...
distancesampling.org <http://distancesampling.org>

